I can confirm that using the re-implementation here [https://github.com/scaomath/galerkin-transformer](https://github.com/scaomath/galerkin-transformer/blob/a9c2dc4427bfaba051d7e0154f110e460050c1df/libs/layers.py#L1040),
- change `nn.Conv1d` to a faster `nn.Linear`;
- remove batch norm, optional layer norm in the wrapper;
- change ReLU to SiLU (similar to GeLU);
(see also the FNO official repo's issue in Review 5dc6's comments)

If we use FNO original repo's dataset. The 1d Burgers benchmark for FNO1d with 500 epochs of `MultiStepLR` with starting LR `1e-3`, weight decay `1e-4`, for 10 seeds the lowest evaluation error is `2.174e-3` with an average of `2.290e-3`.

Plus RNN-like temporal composition will be slow (equation (16) in the paper). No method can dethrone FNO in terms of speed (not surprising since it is FFT + dense matrix multiplication along the temporal direction).
